SUMMARY:Susquehanna Super Hike
DESCRIPTION:Metrotrails: NJ\, PA\, NY Metropolitan Area Hiking and More!\n
 It's been a long time since we've done a more difficult hike like this\, b
 ut it's been a long time coming.\nSince the son is going to "Trunk or Trea
 t" with his mom\, it is an opportunity to complete the final difficult sec
 tion of the Conestoga Trail\, as well as the next bit of the Mason-Dixon T
 rail in our rebooted series that we'd put on hold.\nThis promises to be ch
 allenging\, but also very rewarding with views\, history\, and more.\nAfte
 r meeting at Pequea boat launch\, we will shuttle with as few vehicles as 
 possible to our start point north of the Norman Wood Bridge.\nWe'll follow
  the Mason-Dixon Trail up to an overlook\, and then down through a scenic 
 gorge with small waterfalls. We then get on the towpath of the old Susqueh
 anna and Tidewater Canal to Lock 12. This is the southern terminus of the 
 Conestoga Trail.\nWe'll follow the Conestoga Trail across Norman Wood Brid
 ge\, and then up though lands to the north. Undulating terrain and beautif
 ul vistas are throughout the section.\nPinnacle Rock and House Rock are am
 ong the best views on the entire trail. We'll also pass through lovely Tuc
 quan Glen\, and pass the Wind Cave\, largest tectonic cave east of the Mis
 sissippi.\nThose wanting more terrain and a bit more of a challenge than t
 he other more recent hikes are going to love this one.\nThere are no food 
 stops along the way. In fact\, other than Norman Wood Bridge and two acces
 s roads\, we won't even be crossing any roads on this one.\nDistance will 
 be normal\, just over 15 miles. If needed\, contact me at 908 343 8374
